[{:db/id 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-60af0127-6c17-5939-bada-d5e4db0c560b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119626 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](images/39/39c738068392d3e8f34d72cb46d270e480067d9db8b0353ba1eb05f3a961030c.svg) and ![](images/53/53b09ea4cefbe48a6e1690bc772e74bd59f361bfbc6410a1ae02ac3555233b23.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/ca/ca8ec71c9d54982494530c4458f3a596a3b61a0529acd42c9ddae3fe9c500e34.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119672 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](images/7d/7d5c2f2aaa1ee01159856bcfad7ef1039c169c2fdb96c9c4ab17db5ba9be9067.svg) ![](images/78/784f5d018101808c55cf63b332ae50c9d4fc8e3ded23ad9144e417052d2eaba3.svg) and ![](images/49/491d6bb66fd54ae64e406eef6607526c23c6451a3ed0df837d63170c0b9adb95.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/fd/fd0c93483a1a4386dd0269ac11cbe99567176e1e9d5dbd53477e46cb0704074c.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c63eedb8-35fe-5b46-b979-91cc24ab9428" "question-06d652ec-bdc3-5e47-8845-0d191af934f6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119673 :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n![](images/3f/3ff69817bd8aeb5d8326b7ea9fdc68ceeef52840d4f77ee7dffb9f72514f4532.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n![](images/79/79525b86cbbbc191f9a19ffa817fdaebeb0d1f6bd99eb97ce3f642a7fdf6033e.svg)\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n![](images/c7/c732a274f27d98231f9a94dad40ff15f53e82f00b076ba46464ac640c2765703.svg)\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-60af0127-6c17-5939-bada-d5e4db0c560b" :question/id #uuid "60af0127-6c17-5939-bada-d5e4db0c560b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74970" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71a4b4a1-6fc1-55b1-954d-d7c91a2836db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71a4b4a1-6fc1-55b1-954d-d7c91a2836db" :answer-field/id #uuid "71a4b4a1-6fc1-55b1-954d-d7c91a2836db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0494c3d-162e-5780-b464-32deeb834c29" "answer-b9829215-b974-58e4-8c44-69d894b68143" "answer-f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" "answer-cbf16f8a-df5e-5944-aca2-961b097f9609" "answer-dec9dd50-c1ab-549a-ab1c-6389d8a6d6c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0494c3d-162e-5780-b464-32deeb834c29" :answer/id #uuid "c0494c3d-162e-5780-b464-32deeb834c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9829215-b974-58e4-8c44-69d894b68143" :answer/id #uuid "b9829215-b974-58e4-8c44-69d894b68143" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/id #uuid "f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbf16f8a-df5e-5944-aca2-961b097f9609" :answer/id #uuid "cbf16f8a-df5e-5944-aca2-961b097f9609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec9dd50-c1ab-549a-ab1c-6389d8a6d6c7" :answer/id #uuid "dec9dd50-c1ab-549a-ab1c-6389d8a6d6c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c63eedb8-35fe-5b46-b979-91cc24ab9428" :question/id #uuid "c63eedb8-35fe-5b46-b979-91cc24ab9428" :question/math-academy-id "q-76329" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=14 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-5,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-5)=-10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-065dd387-4e97-5fab-8aff-b38d1bbdcf22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-065dd387-4e97-5fab-8aff-b38d1bbdcf22" :answer-field/id #uuid "065dd387-4e97-5fab-8aff-b38d1bbdcf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" "answer-3a46ae99-d148-5f19-94ba-416fdd09e06f" "answer-8e0cb693-c1d6-5a53-a944-ae0f4704df17" "answer-2d2fc535-0009-527e-8e16-197236ba5926" "answer-b0634bb2-64a1-5d50-8b4f-34a9ef1a9df0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/id #uuid "bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a46ae99-d148-5f19-94ba-416fdd09e06f" :answer/id #uuid "3a46ae99-d148-5f19-94ba-416fdd09e06f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e0cb693-c1d6-5a53-a944-ae0f4704df17" :answer/id #uuid "8e0cb693-c1d6-5a53-a944-ae0f4704df17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d2fc535-0009-527e-8e16-197236ba5926" :answer/id #uuid "2d2fc535-0009-527e-8e16-197236ba5926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0634bb2-64a1-5d50-8b4f-34a9ef1a9df0" :answer/id #uuid "b0634bb2-64a1-5d50-8b4f-34a9ef1a9df0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-06d652ec-bdc3-5e47-8845-0d191af934f6" :question/id #uuid "06d652ec-bdc3-5e47-8845-0d191af934f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76334" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=-6,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-6)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+0+(-6)=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55d04c6b-2631-5950-abad-871e8e80ece8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55d04c6b-2631-5950-abad-871e8e80ece8" :answer-field/id #uuid "55d04c6b-2631-5950-abad-871e8e80ece8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5aaf6733-e757-5737-a6d7-d37f344e2387" "answer-f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" "answer-fed87abb-5218-5d71-9132-58acdc6b2500" "answer-6536472f-623b-557c-ba85-a0b19e98cf60" "answer-a2ba4765-28ed-5cc7-822f-5b6db17d805d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aaf6733-e757-5737-a6d7-d37f344e2387" :answer/id #uuid "5aaf6733-e757-5737-a6d7-d37f344e2387" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/id #uuid "f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fed87abb-5218-5d71-9132-58acdc6b2500" :answer/id #uuid "fed87abb-5218-5d71-9132-58acdc6b2500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6536472f-623b-557c-ba85-a0b19e98cf60" :answer/id #uuid "6536472f-623b-557c-ba85-a0b19e98cf60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2ba4765-28ed-5cc7-822f-5b6db17d805d" :answer/id #uuid "a2ba4765-28ed-5cc7-822f-5b6db17d805d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121040 :tutorial/content "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n![](images/aa/aaa76e82d8d54b305cfcfb0414f1fe7dd4d1c9d3ff932b4206a7b864922b394e.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n![](images/23/237305d1b82f44e0c365f721a33c35d8d983bd5facd4471d1885e8180ec7267e.svg)\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0ff277adbace09315a1c0cd7c964eac41f5e2f080d0e32f888f379be435da0.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n![](images/79/79c529aa2fb5373ff9f8798300b1423ea63f90778bd4832ba1276f6af9c21f04.svg)\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121064 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n![](images/4c/4c12a6e56a6cc7cd72371c59f41b33d8876cf4b98aa40766f0e67ad1bc3a32f7.svg)\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/1d/1dd0ca9bae9a29dea8fc34ed3d9fc453a1d52c48f1f19a95c2fb16c65cd9c7d8.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121065 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n![](images/94/944c607bf892f0d346df79dfa08e6bfec699e64feac018d40a733be0a5c11041.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n![](images/70/70a26164bd64f6e4483b38a08187cac2e1498df7f9066e2be9a295e7b04459da.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/2a/2a92f6d5b67012d342685436d6ccc0ae1670791a060129b10c7d858cafda7b26.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/4d/4defcc004e360ec2e87798b158616575f284ae36369c766ee950c20ae06eff0a.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/1b/1be8cce3195aa633e76e855eba391dd8d1342cf0f3a19e0302eaf67e2828b909.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n![](images/cf/cf5185386d46084579b56fc6ad00769c7c5e7a0d2f320be7ccb28aa4961519ae.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120049 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
